1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,390
Hi. This is Synapse — a learning system that
predicts what you're about

2
00:00:04,480 --> 00:00:06,435
to forget, before you forget it.

3
00:00:06,525 --> 00:00:09,502
In the future of work, people are always
learning:

4
00:00:09,592 --> 00:00:11,547
new tools, new courses, new certifications.

5
00:00:11,637 --> 00:00:13,592
The hard part isn't finding material.

6
00:00:13,682 --> 00:00:17,681
It's knowing what still sticks — and what
is quietly slipping away

7
00:00:17,771 --> 00:00:20,407
before the next deadline or the next shift.

8
00:00:20,527 --> 00:00:24,019
On the landing page, you can already see
the idea.

9
00:00:24,109 --> 00:00:27,631
Synapse turns your own notes into a living
concept map.

10
00:00:27,721 --> 00:00:30,159
Each concept is coloured by predicted retention,

11
00:00:30,249 --> 00:00:34,133
and outlined by how well you understood it
on a quiz.

12
00:00:34,223 --> 00:00:38,467
Memory and understanding stay separate, so
you never confuse I saw this

13
00:00:38,557 --> 00:00:40,634
recently with I actually get it.

14
00:00:40,754 --> 00:00:43,853
I'll sign in with a demo account so you can
see a

15
00:00:43,943 --> 00:00:45,194
real workspace in dark mode.

16
00:00:45,314 --> 00:00:47,064
Here is the home map.

17
00:00:47,154 --> 00:00:49,683
Two course hubs branch into concepts —

18
00:00:49,773 --> 00:00:52,676
nineteen concepts across two courses in this
demo.

19
00:00:52,766 --> 00:00:53,799
Green means fresh.

20
00:00:53,889 --> 00:00:54,921
Red means fading.

21
00:00:55,011 --> 00:00:58,288
Eleven of these are already below fifty percent
retention.

22
00:00:58,378 --> 00:01:03,526
The outline around each node is quiz confidence:
not assessed, low, medium, or high.

23
00:01:03,616 --> 00:01:07,641
When two courses share an idea, Synapse draws
a cross-course link.

24
00:01:07,731 --> 00:01:11,382
What you learn in one place can strengthen
another —

25
00:01:11,472 --> 00:01:14,001
the way skills compound on the job.

26
00:01:14,121 --> 00:01:17,958
On the right, Review next surfaces the most
urgent concepts.

27
00:01:18,048 --> 00:01:20,728
Alpha-Beta Pruning is at about twenty percent.

28
00:01:20,818 --> 00:01:22,707
Overfitting and Regularization at twenty-nine.

29
00:01:22,797 --> 00:01:24,289
Backpropagation is at zero.

30
00:01:24,379 --> 00:01:28,247
Click one to open a short explanation and
review it.

31
00:01:28,337 --> 00:01:31,808
Retention jumps back up, and the next fade
slows.

32
00:01:31,898 --> 00:01:36,556
That is the spacing effect, fitted to your
own review history —

33
00:01:36,646 --> 00:01:38,535
not a generic flashcard schedule.

34
00:01:38,655 --> 00:01:41,964
Upload a course from PDFs, images, or text —

35
00:01:42,054 --> 00:01:45,013
including photographed handwriting — up to
five files.

36
00:01:45,103 --> 00:01:47,680
Name the course, optionally tag a semester,

37
00:01:47,770 --> 00:01:51,872
and Synapse extracts the concepts and builds
the map for you.

38
00:01:51,992 --> 00:01:54,663
The Guide ranks every concept weakest first,

39
00:01:54,753 --> 00:01:57,852
with retention percentages, confidence, recency,
explanations, study resources,

40
00:01:57,942 --> 00:01:59,845
and industry skills per course.

41
00:01:59,935 --> 00:02:04,629
So your next hour of work goes where it matters
most —

42
00:02:04,719 --> 00:02:09,413
whether you're a student, a trainee, or someone
reskilling for a new

43
00:02:09,931 --> 00:02:13,437
For the future of work, continuous learning
is the job.

44
00:02:13,527 --> 00:02:15,974
Synapse doesn't ask people to reread everything.

45
00:02:16,064 --> 00:02:21,049
It shows what is about to fade — so skills
stay sharp without burnout.

46
00:02:21,139 --> 00:02:24,674
That's Synapse. Know what you'll forget before
you forget it.

